(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["anatomy-exam-3-gi-i-ii-quiz"]={"title":"Gastrointestinal Tract I & II — Anatomy Exam 3","category":"Anatomy","questions":[{"q":"The digestive organs that form the continuous tube from mouth to anus are collectively called the GI tract. Which of the following is an accessory digestive organ rather than part of that tube?","choices":["Liver","Stomach","Esophagus","Small intestine"],"answer":0,"exp":"The liver is an accessory digestive organ — it does not form the GI tube but connects to it (by ducts) and assists in digestion. — 'General Structure and Functions of the GI Tract' — accessory organs (Teeth, Tongue, Salivary glands, Liver, Gallbladder, Pancreas)"},{"q":"From the lumen outward, the four tunics of the GI tract wall are arranged in which order?","choices":["Serosa, muscularis, submucosa, mucosa","Mucosa, submucosa, muscularis externa, serosa","Submucosa, mucosa, serosa, muscularis","Mucosa, muscularis, submucosa, serosa"],"answer":1,"exp":"From the lumen outward the layers are mucosa, submucosa, muscularis (externa), then serosa. — 'GI tract tunics' diagram (pp. 10–11)"},{"q":"The muscularis externa of the GI tract consists of which two smooth-muscle layers?","choices":["Inner longitudinal and outer circular","Two circular layers","Inner circular and outer longitudinal","Two longitudinal layers"],"answer":2,"exp":"The muscularis externa has an inner circular layer and an outer longitudinal layer. — 'GI tract tunics' — Muscularis externa (Longitudinal, Circular)"},{"q":"Unlike the rest of the GI tract, the esophagus is covered by which outer layer instead of a serosa?","choices":["Submucosa","Mesentery","Peritoneum","Adventitia"],"answer":3,"exp":"The esophagus is not covered by serosa; its outer layer is adventitia (noted directly on the tunics slide). — 'GI tract tunics' handwritten note — 'esophagus not covered by serosa (adventitia)'"},{"q":"The intrinsic (enteric) innervation of the GI tract is distributed in two intramural plexuses. Which pair is named in the lecture?","choices":["Myenteric and submucosal plexuses","Celiac and mesenteric plexuses","Cardiac and pulmonary plexuses","Sympathetic and parasympathetic trunks"],"answer":0,"exp":"The two intrinsic plexuses are the myenteric nerve plexus and the submucosal nerve plexus. — 'GI tract tunics' — Intrinsic nerve plexuses; 'Special Features' — enteric nervous system"},{"q":"The peritoneum is a serous membrane lining the abdomen and pelvis. Its two named potential spaces are the:","choices":["Pleural and pericardial sacs","Greater sac and lesser sac","Supracolic and infracolic sacs","Right and left subphrenic sacs"],"answer":1,"exp":"The peritoneal cavity contains the greater sac and the lesser sac. — 'Peritoneum' — Greater sac, Lesser sac"},{"q":"The greater omentum extends from which structure and drapes over the transverse colon and small bowel?","choices":["Lesser curvature of the stomach","Porta hepatis","Greater curvature of the stomach","Root of the mesentery"],"answer":2,"exp":"The greater omentum extends from the greater curvature of the stomach and covers the transverse colon and small bowel. — 'Greater Omentum' — extends from greater curvature of stomach"},{"q":"The lesser omentum extends from the porta hepatis to the lesser curvature of the stomach and comprises which two ligaments?","choices":["Falciform and coronary ligaments","Triangular and round ligaments","Splenorenal and gastrosplenic ligaments","Hepatogastric and hepatoduodenal ligaments"],"answer":3,"exp":"The lesser omentum is made of the hepatogastric and hepatoduodenal ligaments. — 'Peritoneal Reflections' — Lesser Omentum"},{"q":"The mesentery proper suspends the jejunum and ileum. Its root runs from the duodenojejunal flexure to which landmark?","choices":["Right iliac fossa","Left iliac fossa","Porta hepatis","Splenic flexure"],"answer":0,"exp":"The root of the mesentery proper runs from the duodenojejunal flexure to the right iliac fossa. — 'Mesentery' — Root from duodenojejunal flexure to right iliac fossa"},{"q":"The mesentery proper carries which structures between its two peritoneal layers?","choices":["Bile and pancreatic ducts","Blood vessels, lymphatics, and nerves","Ureters and gonadal vessels","Cerebrospinal fluid"],"answer":1,"exp":"Blood vessels, lymphatics, and nerves run between the layers of the mesentery proper. — 'Mesentery' — Blood vessels, lymphatics, nerves between layers"},{"q":"Which peritoneal fold is described as extending from the greater curvature and hanging down 'like an apron' over the abdominal viscera?","choices":["Lesser omentum","Transverse mesocolon","Greater omentum","Falciform ligament"],"answer":2,"exp":"The greater omentum hangs like an apron from the greater curvature over the bowel. — 'Omenta' cadaver slide — greater omentum 'hangs like apron'"},{"q":"The lateral walls of the oral cavity are formed by the cheeks, which contain which muscle?","choices":["Masseter","Mylohyoid","Orbicularis oris","Buccinator"],"answer":3,"exp":"The cheeks forming the lateral walls contain the buccinator muscles. — 'Oral Cavity' — lateral walls formed by cheeks, contain buccinator muscles"},{"q":"The lips (labia) are covered by which type of epithelium and contain which muscle?","choices":["Keratinized stratified squamous ET; orbicularis oris","Nonkeratinized ET; buccinator","Simple columnar ET; mylohyoid","Pseudostratified ET; masseter"],"answer":0,"exp":"The lips contain the orbicularis oris muscle and are covered by keratinized stratified squamous epithelium. — 'Oral Cavity' — Lips: orbicularis oris, keratinized stratified squamous ET"},{"q":"The floor (inferior surface) of the oral cavity is formed by the tongue and which muscle covered with mucosa?","choices":["Buccinator muscle","Mylohyoid muscle","Masseter muscle","Digastric muscle"],"answer":1,"exp":"The floor of the oral cavity is formed by the tongue and the mylohyoid muscle covered with mucosa. — 'Oral Cavity (mouth)' — floor: tongue and mylohyoid muscle"},{"q":"The tongue is formed from skeletal muscle and manipulates food to form what during chewing?","choices":["Colloid","The chyme","The bolus","Feces"],"answer":2,"exp":"The tongue mixes ingested materials and forms the bolus, a globular mass of partially digested material. — 'Tongue' — forms the bolus"},{"q":"Which pair of salivary glands is the largest and lies anterior and inferior to the ear, partially overlying the masseter?","choices":["Submandibular glands","Lingual glands","Sublingual glands","Parotid glands"],"answer":3,"exp":"The parotid glands are the largest salivary glands, located anterior/inferior to the ear and overlying the masseter, with a close relationship to the facial nerve. — 'The Parotid Glands'"},{"q":"The parotid gland empties into the oral cavity by way of which duct?","choices":["Stensen's duct","Wharton's duct","Duct of Wirsung","Bartholin's duct"],"answer":0,"exp":"The parotid gland opens by way of the parotid (Stensen's) duct into the oral cavity. — 'The Parotid Glands' — opens by way of Stensen's duct"},{"q":"The esophagus is described in the lecture as having which three portions along its course?","choices":["Cardiac, fundic, pyloric","Cervical, mediastinal, abdominal","Ascending, transverse, descending","Superior, descending, horizontal"],"answer":1,"exp":"The esophagus has cervical, mediastinal (thoracic), and abdominal portions. — 'Esophagus' — Portions: Cervical, Mediastinal, Abdominal"},{"q":"The esophagus passes through the diaphragm at the esophageal hiatus at which vertebral level?","choices":["T8","T12","T10","L1"],"answer":2,"exp":"The esophagus passes through the diaphragm at the esophageal hiatus at the T10 level, terminating at about T11. — 'Esophageal Relationships' (T10); 'Esophagus' — Diaphragm (T10), Termination (T11)"},{"q":"The esophageal mucosa is lined by which epithelium?","choices":["Simple columnar epithelium","Pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium","Simple cuboidal epithelium","Nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium"],"answer":3,"exp":"The esophageal mucosa is lined by nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium. — 'Esophagus' — Histology: Mucosa nonkeratinized stratified squamous ep."},{"q":"The superior esophageal sphincter, located where the pharynx and esophagus meet, is composed of which muscle type?","choices":["Skeletal muscle (cricopharyngeus)","Circular smooth muscle","Cardiac muscle","Elastic connective tissue"],"answer":0,"exp":"The superior esophageal sphincter is skeletal muscle — the cricopharyngeus — where the pharynx and esophagus meet. — 'Esophagus' — Superior esophageal sphincter: skeletal muscle, cricopharyngeus"},{"q":"The inferior esophageal (cardiac) sphincter is described as circular smooth muscle whose function is assisted by which structure, since it is 'not a true sphincter'?","choices":["The liver","The diaphragm","The transverse colon","The greater omentum"],"answer":1,"exp":"The inferior/cardiac sphincter is circular smooth muscle that is not a true sphincter; its function is helped by the diaphragm. — 'Esophagus' — Inferior esophageal sphincter, function helped by diaphragm"},{"q":"Which of the following is the most superior region of the stomach?","choices":["Cardia","Body","Fundus","Pyloric antrum"],"answer":2,"exp":"The fundus is the most superior region of the stomach. — 'The Stomach' — Regions: Cardia, Fundus (most superior), Body, Pyloric"},{"q":"The pyloric region of the stomach is subdivided into which parts?","choices":["Greater and lesser curvatures","Fundus and body","Cardia and notch","Antrum and canal (with the pyloric sphincter)"],"answer":3,"exp":"The pyloric region includes the antrum and the canal, ending at the pyloric sphincter. — 'The Stomach' — Pyloric: Antrum, Canal, Sphincter"},{"q":"The two curvatures of the stomach are the:","choices":["Greater and lesser curvatures","Anterior and posterior curvatures","Superior and inferior curvatures","Medial and lateral curvatures"],"answer":0,"exp":"The stomach has a greater curvature and a lesser curvature. — 'The Stomach' — Curvatures: Greater, Lesser"},{"q":"Which mucosal specialization of the stomach interior consists of longitudinal folds that allow the stomach to expand?","choices":["Plicae circulares","Rugae","Haustra","Villi"],"answer":1,"exp":"Rugae are the longitudinal mucosal folds of the stomach interior (along with the gastric canal, pits, and glands). — 'Stomach Interior' — Mucosal specializations: Rugae, gastric canal, pits, glands"},{"q":"The greater omentum attaches along the greater curvature of the stomach. The lesser omentum attaches along which stomach border?","choices":["Greater curvature","Anterior surface","Lesser curvature","Pyloric antrum"],"answer":2,"exp":"The lesser omentum attaches along the lesser curvature of the stomach (extending to the porta hepatis). — 'Peritoneal Reflections' — lesser omentum to lesser curvature; 'The Stomach' — curvatures"},{"q":"The small intestine extends from the pylorus of the stomach to which structure of the large intestine?","choices":["Sigmoid colon","Splenic flexure","Rectum","Cecum"],"answer":3,"exp":"The small intestine extends from the pylorus of the stomach to the cecum of the large intestine. — 'Small Intestine' — extends from pylorus to cecum"},{"q":"Which segment is the first part of the small intestine, is approximately 25 cm (10 inches) long, and contains the major duodenal papilla?","choices":["Duodenum","Jejunum","Ileum","Cecum"],"answer":0,"exp":"The duodenum is the first segment (~25 cm), originating at the pyloric sphincter and containing the major duodenal papilla. — 'Small Intestine' — The duodenum"},{"q":"The small intestine terminates at the ileocecal valve, a sphincter located at the end of which segment?","choices":["Duodenum","Ileum","Jejunum","Pylorus"],"answer":1,"exp":"The ileum is the last region of the small intestine and terminates at the ileocecal valve. — 'Small Intestine' — The ileum terminates at the ileocecal valve"},{"q":"The plicae circulares of the small intestine are described as:","choices":["Temporary folds that flatten when the intestine fills","Longitudinal folds identical to gastric rugae","Permanent transverse folds that do not disappear when the intestine fills","Sacculations formed by muscle tone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Plicae circulares are permanent transverse folds that do not disappear when the small intestine fills. — 'Plicae' — permanent, do not disappear when small intestine fills"},{"q":"Which portion of the duodenum is the shortest, retroperitoneal, and (for its first part / duodenal cap) intraperitoneal?","choices":["Ascending part","Descending part","Transverse (horizontal) part","Superior part"],"answer":3,"exp":"The superior part is the shortest and widest; its first portion, the duodenal cap (ampulla), is intraperitoneal, while the rest of the duodenum is retroperitoneal. — 'Duodenum' — Superior part / Duodenal cap"},{"q":"Distinguishing features of the jejunum compared with the ileum include which of the following?","choices":["Larger diameter, thicker walls, and more prominent plicae circulares","Smaller diameter and thinner walls","Presence of numerous Peyer's patches","Location mainly in the right lower quadrant"],"answer":0,"exp":"The jejunum has a larger diameter, thicker walls, more plicae circulares, and is more vascular; it lies mostly in the left upper quadrant. — 'Jejunum' — larger diameter, thicker walls, plicae circulares, more vascular"},{"q":"The large intestine is composed of which four segments?","choices":["Duodenum, jejunum, ileum, cecum","Cecum, colon, rectum, anal canal","Fundus, body, antrum, pylorus","Ascending, transverse, descending, sigmoid only"],"answer":1,"exp":"The large intestine is composed of the cecum, colon, rectum, and anal canal. — 'Large Intestine' — cecum, colon, rectum, anal canal"},{"q":"Which three anatomic specializations are characteristic of the colon but absent in the rectum and appendix?","choices":["Villi, plicae, rugae","Peyer's patches, crypts, microvilli","Taeniae coli, haustra (sacculations), and omental appendices","Fundus, antrum, pylorus"],"answer":2,"exp":"The colon shows three taeniae coli, haustra (sacculations), and omental appendices — features absent in the rectum and appendix. — 'Large Intestine' — Taeniae coli (3), sacculations (haustra), omental appendices"},{"q":"The appendix (vermiform appendix) is a slender hollow appendage anchored by which structure and dominated by which tissue?","choices":["Phrenicocolic ligament; villi","Transverse mesocolon; goblet cells","Falciform ligament; smooth muscle","Mesoappendix; lymphoid nodules"],"answer":3,"exp":"The appendix is fixed by the mesoappendix and is dominated by lymphoid nodules (a lymphoid organ). — 'Cecum and Appendix'; 'The Appendix' — mesoappendix, lymphoid nodules"},{"q":"The ascending colon terminates at which flexure?","choices":["Right colic (hepatic) flexure","Left colic (splenic) flexure","Duodenojejunal flexure","Sigmoid flexure"],"answer":0,"exp":"The ascending colon terminates at the right colic (hepatic) flexure. — 'Ascending Colon' — terminates at right colic (hepatic) flexure"},{"q":"Which segment of the colon is described as the most mobile and longest, is intraperitoneal, and is suspended by the transverse mesocolon?","choices":["Ascending colon","Transverse colon","Descending colon","Sigmoid colon"],"answer":1,"exp":"The transverse colon is the most mobile and longest portion, is intraperitoneal, and is suspended by the transverse mesocolon between the right and left colic flexures. — 'Transverse Colon'"},{"q":"The liver is the largest visceral organ and largest gland. It is completely covered by which capsule?","choices":["Gerota's fascia","Bowman's capsule","Glisson's capsule","Tenon's capsule"],"answer":2,"exp":"The liver is completely covered by Glisson's capsule (with incomplete peritoneal covering). — 'The Liver' — completely covered by Glisson's Capsule"},{"q":"The liver receives a triple blood supply. Which vessel delivers the inflow of blood needing metabolic processing?","choices":["Hepatic artery","Cystic artery","Hepatic veins","Portal vein"],"answer":3,"exp":"The portal vein delivers inflow of blood needing metabolic processing to the liver. — 'Liver triple blood supply' — Portal vein"},{"q":"The caudate lobe of the liver lies between the inferior vena cava and which structure?","choices":["Ligamentum venosum","Ligamentum teres","Gallbladder","Falciform ligament"],"answer":0,"exp":"The caudate lobe lies between the inferior vena cava and the ligamentum venosum. — 'Lobes of the Liver' — Caudate: between IVC and ligamentum venosum"},{"q":"The quadrate lobe of the liver is located between which two structures?","choices":["The IVC and the ligamentum venosum","The gallbladder and the ligamentum teres","The falciform and coronary ligaments","The porta hepatis and the bare area"],"answer":1,"exp":"The quadrate lobe lies between the gallbladder and the ligamentum teres (round ligament). — 'Lobes of the Liver' — Quadrate: between gall bladder and ligamentum teres"},{"q":"The portal triad — found at the corners of a liver lobule — consists of branches of which three structures?","choices":["Hepatic artery, cystic duct, and central vein","Central vein, hepatic vein, and lymphatic","Portal vein, hepatic artery, and bile duct","Portal vein, hepatic vein, and pancreatic duct"],"answer":2,"exp":"The portal triad consists of a branch of the portal vein, hepatic artery, and bile duct. — 'Histology of the liver' — Portal triad: portal vein, hepatic artery, bile duct"},{"q":"The pancreas is described as retroperitoneal with which regions?","choices":["Cardia, fundus, body, pylorus","Fundus, body, neck","Superior, descending, horizontal, ascending","Head, uncinate process, neck, body, and tail"],"answer":3,"exp":"The pancreas has a head, uncinate process, neck, body, and tail. — 'The Pancreas' — Regions: Head, Uncinate process, Neck, body, tail"},{"q":"The main pancreatic duct is also known by which eponym?","choices":["Duct of Wirsung","Duct of Santorini","Duct of Wharton","Duct of Stensen"],"answer":0,"exp":"The principal (main) pancreatic duct is the duct of Wirsung; the accessory duct is the duct of Santorini. — 'Pancreas' — Principal pancreatic duct (of Wirsung), accessory (of Santorini)"},{"q":"The pancreatic islets, the endocrine tissue of the pancreas, make up approximately what fraction of pancreatic cells?","choices":["About 25%","About 1%","About 50%","About 75%"],"answer":1,"exp":"The pancreatic islets are scattered endocrine tissue making up about 1% of pancreatic cells. — 'Pancreatic Islets' — scattered (1% of pancreatic cells)"},{"q":"The celiac trunk arises from the aorta at the aortic hiatus and gives which three branches?","choices":["Right gastric, cystic, and gastroduodenal arteries","Superior mesenteric, inferior mesenteric, and renal arteries","Left gastric, splenic, and common hepatic arteries","Splenic, superior mesenteric, and left colic arteries"],"answer":2,"exp":"The celiac trunk branches into the left gastric, splenic, and common hepatic arteries. — 'Arterial Supply of the Abdomen — The Celiac Trunk'"},{"q":"The common hepatic artery continues as the proper hepatic artery and also gives off which branch that descends toward the duodenum and pancreas?","choices":["Left gastric artery","Superior mesenteric artery","Splenic artery","Gastroduodenal artery"],"answer":3,"exp":"The common hepatic artery gives off the gastroduodenal artery (and the proper hepatic continues to the liver, giving right gastric and right/left hepatic branches). — 'Common Hepatic Artery' — Proper hepatic, Right gastric, Gastroduodenal, Supraduodenal"},{"q":"Which artery supplying the esophagus is a branch of the celiac artery?","choices":["Left gastric branch of the celiac artery","Inferior thyroid branch of the thyrocervical trunk","Bronchial arteries","Internal thoracic artery"],"answer":0,"exp":"The left gastric branch of the celiac artery supplies the (abdominal) esophagus. — 'Esophagus' — arterial supply (left gastric branch of the celiac artery)"},{"q":"The hepatic veins that drain the liver empty directly into which vessel?","choices":["Portal vein","Inferior vena cava","Splenic vein","Superior mesenteric vein"],"answer":1,"exp":"The hepatic veins drain into the inferior vena cava, carrying outflow toward the right atrium. — 'Histology of the liver' — Hepatic veins drain into inferior vena cava; 'Liver triple blood supply'"}]};
